  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Cauchy's Integral Formula [S+N]\n\nMost of complex analysis runs through this formula. If $f$ is holomorphic on an open set containing a closed disc $\\overline{D_R(z_0)}$, then for any $z$ in the interior\n\n$$f(z) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi i} \\oint_{|\\zeta - z_0| = R} \\frac{f(\\zeta)}{\\zeta - z}\\,d\\zeta.$$\n\nIn words: **values of a holomorphic function inside a disc are determined by its values on the boundary.** Smooth real functions have no such property: $C^\\infty$ on $\\mathbb{R}$ gives no boundary-value control.\n\nThree consequences land immediately:\n\n1. **Holomorphic ⇒ infinitely differentiable.** Differentiate the integrand under the integral sign.\n2. **Holomorphic ⇒ analytic.** The function equals its Taylor series locally.\n3. **Maximum modulus principle.** $|f|$ on a domain attains its max on the boundary.\n\nThis notebook walks the formula and these consequences.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 29,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Verifying the formula numerically [S+N]\n\nTake $f(z) = z^2 + 1$ (entire) and $z_0 = 0$, $R = 1$. The formula at $z = 0.3 + 0.4i$ predicts $f(0.3 + 0.4i) = (0.3 + 0.4i)^2 + 1$.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "f_eval(z) := z^2 + 1$\nz_check : 0.3 + 0.4 * %i$\ntruth_val : expand(f_eval(z_check))$\nprint(\"f(0.3 + 0.4i) =\", truth_val, \"  (=\", float(realpart(truth_val)),\n      \"+\", float(imagpart(truth_val)), \"i)\")$\n\n/* Approximate the contour integral on |ζ| = 1 numerically */\nN_pts : 2000$\ndt : 2 * float(%pi) / N_pts$\n/* rectform keeps each running total a plain a + b·i float; without it Maxima\n   builds an ever-growing unevaluated expression. */\ntotal : 0$\nfor k : 0 thru N_pts - 1 do block([t, zeta, num, den],\n  t : k * dt,\n  zeta : rectform(exp(%i * t)),\n  num : f_eval(zeta) * %i * zeta,\n  den : zeta - z_check,\n  total : rectform(total + num / den * dt))$\nformula_val : rectform(total / (2 * float(%pi) * %i))$\nprint(\"Cauchy formula prediction:\", formula_val)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 30,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "f(0.3 + 0.4i) = 0.24*%i+0.9299999999999999   (= 0.9299999999999999 + 0.24 i)\nCauchy formula prediction: 0.24000000000000013*%i+0.9300000000000002"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Cauchy's formula for derivatives\n\nDifferentiate under the integral sign:\n\n$$f^{(n)}(z) = \\frac{n!}{2\\pi i} \\oint \\frac{f(\\zeta)}{(\\zeta - z)^{n+1}}\\,d\\zeta.$$\n\nIn particular, **all derivatives exist** as soon as one does. For $f(z) = z^2 + 1$ at $z = 0$, $f''(0) = 2$. Verify:\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Numerical: f''(0) = 2! / (2πi) ∮ f(ζ)/ζ³ dζ */\ntotal2 : 0$\nfor k : 0 thru N_pts - 1 do block([t, zeta, num, den],\n  t : k * dt,\n  zeta : rectform(exp(%i * t)),\n  num : f_eval(zeta) * %i * zeta,\n  den : zeta^3,\n  total2 : rectform(total2 + num / den * dt))$\nfpp_val : rectform(2.0 * total2 / (2 * float(%pi) * %i))$\nprint(\"f''(0) via Cauchy formula:\", fpp_val)$\nprint(\"(true f''(0) = 2)\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 31,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "f''(0) via Cauchy formula: 3.8979352434670644e-17*%i+2.0000000000000053\n(true f''(0) = 2)"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Bounds: the Cauchy estimate\n\nIf $|f| \\le M$ on $|\\zeta - z_0| = R$, then\n\n$$|f^{(n)}(z_0)| \\le \\frac{n!\\,M}{R^n}.$$\n\nThis is the ML inequality applied to the formula for derivatives.\n\n**Liouville's theorem** falls out: a bounded entire function ($f$ holomorphic on all of $\\mathbb{C}$, $|f| \\le M$ globally) has $|f^{(n)}(z_0)| \\le n!\\,M / R^n$ for arbitrarily large $R$, so $f^{(n)}(z_0) = 0$ for all $n \\ge 1$. Thus $f$ is constant.\n\n**Fundamental theorem of algebra** is a one-line corollary: if $p(z)$ is a non-constant polynomial with no zeros, $1/p$ would be entire and bounded — by Liouville, constant. Contradiction.\n\n## Maximum modulus principle\n\nIf $f$ is holomorphic on a connected open set $\\Omega$ and $|f|$ has a *local maximum* in $\\Omega$, then $f$ is constant. Equivalently: **on a bounded domain, $|f|$ attains its maximum on the boundary**.\n\nGeometrically: a holomorphic function can't have an interior peak in $|f|$ unless it's flat. This is a consequence of Cauchy's integral formula too — average of $f$ around a small circle equals the centre value, which forces no interior local max.\n\nDemonstrate by plotting $|f(z)|$ for $f(z) = z^2 + 1$ over the square $[-1, 1]^2$:\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "ax_draw3d(\n  explicit(cabs((x + y*%i)^2 + 1), x, -1, 1, y, -1, 1),\n  title=\"|z² + 1| on the square [-1,1]²: maximum on the boundary (the corners)\",\n  xlabel=\"Re z\", ylabel=\"Im z\", zlabel=\"|f(z)|\",\n  colorscale=\"Viridis\"\n)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 32,
      "outputs": [
        {
